(2,'🟢',"Trunk-based development versus Gitflow for an ML team that runs many experiments. Explain short-lived branches, feature flags, long-lived experiment branches and their cost, how releases and hotfixes work in each model, and which you would pick for a team of 6 shipping a model every two weeks. Say how experiment code that never merges is kept (tags or an archive branch) without cluttering main.",GIT),
(2,'🟢',"What goes into a pull request review checklist for ML code? List at least eight items specific to ML (data leakage, fixed seeds, hard-coded paths, metric on the right split, untracked data version, large diffs from notebooks, missing tests, silent shape or dtype changes), say which a linter could catch, and review a 25-line training function with three planted bugs, naming each bug.",MLO),
(2,'🟡',"Cherry-pick and hotfix backports. Version 2.3 of a scoring service is in production, main has moved on to 2.4 work, and a one-line fix to a null-handling bug landed on main. Show how you backport just that commit to a release/2.3 branch with git cherry-pick, what happens when the commit conflicts, why cherry-picking creates a duplicate commit with a new hash, and how to keep track of which fixes went where. Demonstrate in a throwaway repo.",GIT),
(2,'🟡',"Make randomness testable. A function train_test_split_and_fit(df, seed) gives slightly different metrics on every run. Explain seeding Python, NumPy (np.random.default_rng) and sklearn random_state, the limits of seeding (multi-threaded libraries, GPU kernels), and how to assert on floats with pytest.approx or np.testing.assert_allclose with a justified tolerance. Show a flaky test and the fixed version, both run.",TST),
(2,'🟡',"pytest features used in ML test suites: fixtures (including scope), parametrize, tmp_path, monkeypatch and markers. Write a conftest.py fixture that builds a small seeded DataFrame once per session, a test that uses tmp_path to check a save and load round trip of a model artifact, and a test that monkeypatches a slow model-download function. Say how you mark and skip slow tests (--runslow style) and show the run.",TST),
(2,'🟡',"Data validation tests as code. Write a validate(df) function (using pydantic, plain pandas or pandera, your choice, say why) that checks columns and dtypes, non-null keys, a value range, allowed category values and uniqueness of an id, and reports all violations at once instead of stopping at the first. Test it on a good frame and a frame with four planted problems, and say where in the pipeline it runs (ingest, before training, before serving) and what happens on failure.",TST),
(2,'🟡',"Write tests that catch data leakage. Using seeded data, show a leaky pipeline (scaler fitted on all rows, or duplicate rows across train and test, or a target-derived feature) and the tests that expose each: no id overlap between train and test, scaler statistics equal to train-only statistics, and an unrealistically high score alarm. Run the leaky and fixed versions with scikit-learn and report both scores.",TST),
(2,'🟡',"Property-based testing with Hypothesis for ML utilities. Write properties for a min-max scaler and a train/test splitter (output in [0,1] when the input is non-constant, order of rows preserved or not, sizes add up, no row lost or duplicated) and let Hypothesis find a failing edge case in a deliberately naive implementation (constant column, a single row, NaN or inf). Run it and show the shrunk counterexample. Say when this is worth the effort versus example tests.",TST),
(2,'🟡',"Test coverage in ML repos. A repo reports 92 percent line coverage but a bad scaling bug shipped. Explain line versus branch coverage, why coverage does not measure assertions or data coverage, what a sensible target and a diff-coverage rule look like, and how to run coverage with pytest-cov and fail under a threshold. Show a tiny function where 100 percent line coverage still misses a bug, with the run.",TST),
(2,'🟡',"Use git bisect to find the commit that broke the model. Accuracy dropped between a good tag v1.0 and the current HEAD across 12 commits. Show a throwaway repo with a deterministic test script, then run git bisect start/good/bad and git bisect run to locate the first bad commit and count the steps. Say how you handle commits that cannot build (git bisect skip) and why a flaky test breaks bisect.",GIT),
(2,'🟡',"Git hooks and the pre-commit framework for data science teams. Configure hooks that strip notebook outputs, block files larger than 5 MB, detect obvious secrets in staged changes (a regex-based hook, hedge on tools) and run ruff. Show the .pre-commit-config.yaml, a commit that gets blocked and one that passes, and explain how the same checks must also run in CI because local hooks can be bypassed.",MLO),
(2,'🟡',"Versioning and tagging a model package. Explain semantic versioning for a library that wraps a model (what is a major, minor and patch change when the feature set or the output scale changes), how you tag a release in Git, how the model artifact version is linked to the code tag and the data version, and how you roll back. Show git tag -a, a version string read at runtime from package metadata (importlib.metadata) and a changelog entry, run in a temp repo.",PKG),
(2,'🟡',"Configuration management for ML code. A training script has learning rate, paths and feature lists hard-coded. Move them to a YAML file validated by a pydantic model, with environment overrides and a CLI flag, and fail fast on unknown keys or wrong types. Show the code with a good and a bad config run, say how you log the resolved config with each run, and when a config framework such as Hydra is worth it (hedge, concept only).",MLO),
(2,'🟡',"Logging and error handling in ML pipelines. Replace print debugging in a batch scoring function with the logging module: levels, a structured JSON log line with run id and row counts, raising specific exceptions on bad input versus logging and continuing, and never swallowing exceptions in a bare except. Show the code, its output on a good and a bad batch, and what you would alert on.",MLO),
(2,'🟡',"Type hints and mypy for data and ML code. Explain what type hints buy you in a pandas and NumPy codebase (documentation, catching wrong arguments, IDE help) and what they cannot check (DataFrame columns, array shapes). Annotate a small feature function with typing and numpy.typing, run mypy on a version with a planted type error, and mention gradual adoption (strict only for new modules) and runtime checking with pydantic at the boundaries.",PKG),
(2,'🔴',"Monorepo versus polyrepo for ML platform code. A company has 8 model repos that each copy a feature library. Compare a monorepo, a polyrepo with a shared versioned package, and git submodules (cost, CI time, ownership with CODEOWNERS, breaking changes, release cadence). Recommend one for 8 teams and describe how a breaking change to the shared library is rolled out without breaking the other repos.",X),
