(1,'🟡',"git stash and git worktree for ML work. A training script is mid-edit with uncommitted changes while a production hotfix is needed on main, and a 6-hour run is reading files from the working directory. Explain stash (and its limits, untracked files with -u), and show how git worktree lets you check out the hotfix branch in a second directory without disturbing the run. Demonstrate in a throwaway repo.",GIT),
(1,'🟡',"Repository governance for a model repo: branch protection, required reviews, CODEOWNERS, required status checks, signed commits and tags. Say what each protects against (an unreviewed change to the feature code reaching production, forged authorship), what the GitHub or GitLab settings are called (hedge: check current docs), and show a CODEOWNERS file that makes the data scientist team own models/ and the platform team own deploy/ and parse it for a given changed path in Python.",X),
(1,'🔴',"Flaky tests in ML. A test asserts accuracy greater than 0.90 and fails about 1 run in 20. Diagnose the cause (unseeded split, small test set, nondeterministic library), compare fixes (seed everything, widen the tolerance using a binomial standard error on a 200-row test set, use a larger fixed fixture, test the property rather than the number) and show a run where you measure the failure rate before and after over 200 seeded repetitions with scikit-learn.",TST),
(1,'🔴',"Performance regression gate in CI. A new commit changes a feature and AUC on a fixed 5,000-row validation set falls from 0.874 to 0.869. Should the build fail? Design the gate: a stored baseline file, a threshold, a bootstrap confidence interval for the difference (paired on the same rows), slice checks, and an override process. Implement the bootstrap comparison on seeded synthetic scores with numpy and scikit-learn and report the decision for a small and a real degradation.",TST),
